{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Table is a headless table library. It manages your table's state and logic (sorting, filtering, pagination, selection, and more) while you keep 100% control over the markup and styles. This page gets you from install to a rendering Alpine table, then shows how to layer on your first feature."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"installation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Installation"}]},{"type":"code","value":"npm install @tanstack/alpine-table alpinejs","lang":"bash"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/alpine-table"},{"type":"text","value":" package works with Alpine 3."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"how-the-alpine-adapter-works","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How the Alpine adapter works"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The adapter is built around two ideas:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTable"},{"type":"text","value":" returns a reactive table instance."}]},{"type":"text","value":" State lives in "},{"type":"link","href":"https://tanstack.com/store/latest","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Store"}]},{"type":"text","value":" atoms that the adapter bridges into Alpine's reactivity. Any Alpine binding that reads a table API ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.getRowModel()"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.atoms.sorting.get()"},{"type":"text","value":", and so on) re-runs automatically when the underlying state changes. There is no state selector to pass."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You render with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x-html"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.FlexRender"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"text","value":" A column's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cell"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"header"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"footer"},{"type":"text","value":" renderer returns a string of HTML. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.FlexRender({ cell })"},{"type":"text","value":" produces that string and you place it with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x-html"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"callout","kind":"important","title":"Important","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Alpine does not initialize directives ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@click"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x-model"},{"type":"text","value":", etc.) inside content set with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x-html"},{"type":"text","value":". So render cell/header "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"content"}]},{"type":"text","value":" with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x-html=\"table.FlexRender(...)\""},{"type":"text","value":", but put any "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"interactivity"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (click-to-sort, filter inputs, checkboxes) on real elements in your markup, next to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x-html"},{"type":"text","value":" span. You will see this pattern in the sorting example below."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"your-first-table","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Your First Table"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You define the table in a JavaScript module with "},{"type":"link","href":"https://alpinejs.dev/globals/alpine-data","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Alpine.data"}]},{"type":"text","value":", then render it from your HTML."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// main.ts\nimport Alpine from 'alpinejs'\nimport { FlexRender, createTable, tableFeatures } from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\nimport type { ColumnDef } from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\n\n// 1. Define the shape of your data\ntype Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n}\n\nconst defaultData: Array<Person> = [\n  { firstName: 'tanner', lastName: 'linsley', age: 24 },\n  { firstName: 'tandy', lastName: 'miller', age: 40 },\n  { firstName: 'joe', lastName: 'dirte', age: 45 },\n]\n\n// 2. New in v9: declare which features this table uses (none yet)\nconst features = tableFeatures({})\n\n// 3. Define your columns. Renderers return HTML strings (rendered via x-html).\nconst columns: Array<ColumnDef<typeof features, Person>> = [\n  {\n    accessorKey: 'firstName', // accessorKey shorthand\n    header: 'First Name',\n    cell: (info) => info.getValue(),\n  },\n  {\n    accessorFn: (row) => row.lastName, // accessorFn alternative with a custom id\n    id: 'lastName',\n    header: () => '<span>Last Name</span>',\n    cell: (info) => `<i>${info.getValue<string>()}</i>`,\n  },\n  {\n    accessorKey: 'age',\n    header: () => 'Age',\n  },\n]\n\n// 4. Register an Alpine component\nAlpine.data('table', () => {\n  // Store data in Alpine-reactive state so updates flow into the table\n  const local = Alpine.reactive({ data: defaultData })\n\n  // 5. Create the table instance, reading data through a getter so it stays reactive\n  const table = createTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    get data() {\n      return local.data\n    },\n  })\n\n  // Expose the instance (and FlexRender) to the template\n  return { table, FlexRender }\n})\n\nwindow.Alpine = Alpine\nAlpine.start()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"<!-- index.html -->\n<div x-data=\"table\">\n  <table>\n    <thead>\n      <template\n        x-for=\"headerGroup in table.getHeaderGroups()\"\n        :key=\"headerGroup.id\"\n      >\n        <tr>\n          <template x-for=\"header in headerGroup.headers\" :key=\"header.id\">\n            <th>\n              <template x-if=\"!header.isPlaceholder\">\n                <span x-html=\"FlexRender({ header })\"></span>\n              </template>\n            </th>\n          </template>\n        </tr>\n      </template>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <template x-for=\"row in table.getRowModel().rows\" :key=\"row.id\">\n        <tr>\n          <template x-for=\"cell in row.getAllCells()\" :key=\"cell.id\">\n            <td x-html=\"FlexRender({ cell })\"></td>\n          </template>\n        </tr>\n      </template>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n<script type=\"module\" src=\"/main.ts\"></script>","lang":"html"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A few things to note:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures({})"},{"type":"text","value":" declares which optional features the table uses. Registering only what you need keeps bundles small and gives TypeScript accurate types for the table instance."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The core row model is always included automatically. Feature row models (sorting, filtering, pagination) are registered as slots directly on the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures({...})"},{"type":"text","value":" call when you need them."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"get data()"},{"type":"text","value":" getter keeps the table reactive. When "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"local.data"},{"type":"text","value":" is reassigned, the table sees the new data. Passing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data: local.data"},{"type":"text","value":" would capture a one-time snapshot."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"FlexRender"},{"type":"text","value":" is also attached to the instance as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.FlexRender"},{"type":"text","value":", so you can write "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x-html=\"table.FlexRender({ cell })\""},{"type":"text","value":" instead of exposing the top-level helper."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See the full "},{"type":"link","href":"./examples/basic-create-table","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic createTable example"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for a runnable version with more columns and a footer."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"add-a-feature-sorting","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Add a Feature: Sorting"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Features are opt-in in v9. To make columns sortable, register "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rowSortingFeature"},{"type":"text","value":" and the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sortedRowModel"},{"type":"text","value":" factory in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":", then wire a header click handler. Because the click handler cannot live inside "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x-html"},{"type":"text","value":", wrap the rendered header in a real element and attach "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@click"},{"type":"text","value":" there."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// main.ts (additions)\nimport {\n  FlexRender,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  createTable,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortFns,\n  tableFeatures,\n} from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature, // enables sorting APIs and state\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(), // client-side sorting\n  sortFns,\n})\n\n// columns and the Alpine.data registration are otherwise unchanged","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"<!-- index.html: the <thead> becomes -->\n<thead>\n  <template\n    x-for=\"headerGroup in table.getHeaderGroups()\"\n    :key=\"headerGroup.id\"\n  >\n    <tr>\n      <template x-for=\"header in headerGroup.headers\" :key=\"header.id\">\n        <th>\n          <template x-if=\"!header.isPlaceholder\">\n            <div\n              :style=\"header.column.getCanSort() ? 'cursor: pointer' : ''\"\n              @click=\"header.column.getToggleSortingHandler()?.($event)\"\n            >\n              <span x-html=\"FlexRender({ header })\"></span\n              ><span\n                x-text=\"({ asc: ' 🔼', desc: ' 🔽' })[header.column.getIsSorted()] ?? ''\"\n              ></span>\n            </div>\n          </template>\n        </th>\n      </template>\n    </tr>\n  </template>\n</thead>","lang":"html"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Clicking a header now toggles between ascending, descending, and unsorted. Every other feature follows this same pattern: register the feature (and its row model factory as a slot on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableFeatures"},{"type":"text","value":" if it has one), then use the APIs it adds to the table, columns, and rows, attaching any interactive controls to real elements in your markup. See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./examples/sorting","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sorting example"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for custom sort functions, multi-sorting, and per-column options."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"where-to-go-next","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Where to Go Next"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table state."}]},{"type":"text","value":" In v9, table state is backed by TanStack Store atoms, which the adapter makes reactive in Alpine. You usually do not need to manage it yourself. Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":" for starting values and call feature APIs like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.setSorting(...)"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.nextPage()"},{"type":"text","value":". When you need to read a state slice in your markup, use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.atoms.<slice>.get()"},{"type":"text","value":" (for example "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.atoms.pagination.get().pageIndex"},{"type":"text","value":") or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.store.get()"},{"type":"text","value":" for the whole state. There is no state selector, because the table instance is already reactive. The "},{"type":"link","href":"./guide/table-state.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table State Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" is the foundational guide for everything else."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Feature examples."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Each feature has a runnable example, such as "},{"type":"link","href":"./examples/filters","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Filters"}]},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"link","href":"./examples/pagination","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pagination"}]},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"link","href":"./examples/row-selection","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Row Selection"}]},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"link","href":"./examples/column-visibility","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Visibility"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Composable tables."}]},{"type":"text","value":" When multiple tables in your app share features and row models, define them once with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTableHook"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  sortFns,\n})\n\nconst { createAppTable, createAppColumnHelper } = createTableHook({ features })","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Then call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createAppTable({ columns, data })"},{"type":"text","value":" from your component instead of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createTable"},{"type":"text","value":", and define columns with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createAppColumnHelper"},{"type":"text","value":". See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./examples/basic-app-table","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic createAppTable example"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the full pattern."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Examples."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Browse the runnable "},{"type":"link","href":"./examples/basic-create-table","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Alpine examples"}]},{"type":"text","value":", from basic tables to feature demos, to see intended usage end to end."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"installation","text":"Installation","level":2},{"id":"how-the-alpine-adapter-works","text":"How the Alpine adapter works","level":2},{"id":"your-first-table","text":"Your First Table","level":2},{"id":"add-a-feature-sorting","text":"Add a Feature: Sorting","level":2},{"id":"where-to-go-next","text":"Where to Go Next","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nTanStack Table is a headless table library. It manages your table's state and logic (sorting, filtering, pagination, selection, and more) while you keep 100% control over the markup and styles. This page gets you from install to a rendering Alpine table, then shows how to layer on your first feature.\n\n## Installation\n\n```bash\nnpm install @tanstack/alpine-table alpinejs\n```\n\nThe `@tanstack/alpine-table` package works with Alpine 3.\n\n## How the Alpine adapter works\n\nThe adapter is built around two ideas:\n\n- **`createTable` returns a reactive table instance.** State lives in [TanStack Store](https://tanstack.com/store/latest) atoms that the adapter bridges into Alpine's reactivity. Any Alpine binding that reads a table API (`table.getRowModel()`, `table.atoms.sorting.get()`, and so on) re-runs automatically when the underlying state changes. There is no state selector to pass.\n- **You render with `x-html` and `table.FlexRender`.** A column's `cell`/`header`/`footer` renderer returns a string of HTML. `table.FlexRender({ cell })` produces that string and you place it with `x-html`.\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> Alpine does not initialize directives (`@click`, `x-model`, etc.) inside content set with `x-html`. So render cell/header **content** with `x-html=\"table.FlexRender(...)\"`, but put any **interactivity** (click-to-sort, filter inputs, checkboxes) on real elements in your markup, next to the `x-html` span. You will see this pattern in the sorting example below.\n\n## Your First Table\n\nYou define the table in a JavaScript module with [`Alpine.data`](https://alpinejs.dev/globals/alpine-data), then render it from your HTML.\n\n```ts\n// main.ts\nimport Alpine from 'alpinejs'\nimport { FlexRender, createTable, tableFeatures } from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\nimport type { ColumnDef } from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\n\n// 1. Define the shape of your data\ntype Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n}\n\nconst defaultData: Array<Person> = [\n  { firstName: 'tanner', lastName: 'linsley', age: 24 },\n  { firstName: 'tandy', lastName: 'miller', age: 40 },\n  { firstName: 'joe', lastName: 'dirte', age: 45 },\n]\n\n// 2. New in v9: declare which features this table uses (none yet)\nconst features = tableFeatures({})\n\n// 3. Define your columns. Renderers return HTML strings (rendered via x-html).\nconst columns: Array<ColumnDef<typeof features, Person>> = [\n  {\n    accessorKey: 'firstName', // accessorKey shorthand\n    header: 'First Name',\n    cell: (info) => info.getValue(),\n  },\n  {\n    accessorFn: (row) => row.lastName, // accessorFn alternative with a custom id\n    id: 'lastName',\n    header: () => '<span>Last Name</span>',\n    cell: (info) => `<i>${info.getValue<string>()}</i>`,\n  },\n  {\n    accessorKey: 'age',\n    header: () => 'Age',\n  },\n]\n\n// 4. Register an Alpine component\nAlpine.data('table', () => {\n  // Store data in Alpine-reactive state so updates flow into the table\n  const local = Alpine.reactive({ data: defaultData })\n\n  // 5. Create the table instance, reading data through a getter so it stays reactive\n  const table = createTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    get data() {\n      return local.data\n    },\n  })\n\n  // Expose the instance (and FlexRender) to the template\n  return { table, FlexRender }\n})\n\nwindow.Alpine = Alpine\nAlpine.start()\n```\n\n```html\n<!-- index.html -->\n<div x-data=\"table\">\n  <table>\n    <thead>\n      <template\n        x-for=\"headerGroup in table.getHeaderGroups()\"\n        :key=\"headerGroup.id\"\n      >\n        <tr>\n          <template x-for=\"header in headerGroup.headers\" :key=\"header.id\">\n            <th>\n              <template x-if=\"!header.isPlaceholder\">\n                <span x-html=\"FlexRender({ header })\"></span>\n              </template>\n            </th>\n          </template>\n        </tr>\n      </template>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <template x-for=\"row in table.getRowModel().rows\" :key=\"row.id\">\n        <tr>\n          <template x-for=\"cell in row.getAllCells()\" :key=\"cell.id\">\n            <td x-html=\"FlexRender({ cell })\"></td>\n          </template>\n        </tr>\n      </template>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n<script type=\"module\" src=\"/main.ts\"></script>\n```\n\nA few things to note:\n\n- `tableFeatures({})` declares which optional features the table uses. Registering only what you need keeps bundles small and gives TypeScript accurate types for the table instance.\n- The core row model is always included automatically. Feature row models (sorting, filtering, pagination) are registered as slots directly on the `tableFeatures({...})` call when you need them.\n- The `get data()` getter keeps the table reactive. When `local.data` is reassigned, the table sees the new data. Passing `data: local.data` would capture a one-time snapshot.\n- `FlexRender` is also attached to the instance as `table.FlexRender`, so you can write `x-html=\"table.FlexRender({ cell })\"` instead of exposing the top-level helper.\n\nSee the full [Basic createTable example](./examples/basic-create-table) for a runnable version with more columns and a footer.\n\n## Add a Feature: Sorting\n\nFeatures are opt-in in v9. To make columns sortable, register `rowSortingFeature` and the `sortedRowModel` factory in `tableFeatures`, then wire a header click handler. Because the click handler cannot live inside `x-html`, wrap the rendered header in a real element and attach `@click` there.\n\n```ts\n// main.ts (additions)\nimport {\n  FlexRender,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  createTable,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortFns,\n  tableFeatures,\n} from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature, // enables sorting APIs and state\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(), // client-side sorting\n  sortFns,\n})\n\n// columns and the Alpine.data registration are otherwise unchanged\n```\n\n```html\n<!-- index.html: the <thead> becomes -->\n<thead>\n  <template\n    x-for=\"headerGroup in table.getHeaderGroups()\"\n    :key=\"headerGroup.id\"\n  >\n    <tr>\n      <template x-for=\"header in headerGroup.headers\" :key=\"header.id\">\n        <th>\n          <template x-if=\"!header.isPlaceholder\">\n            <div\n              :style=\"header.column.getCanSort() ? 'cursor: pointer' : ''\"\n              @click=\"header.column.getToggleSortingHandler()?.($event)\"\n            >\n              <span x-html=\"FlexRender({ header })\"></span\n              ><span\n                x-text=\"({ asc: ' 🔼', desc: ' 🔽' })[header.column.getIsSorted()] ?? ''\"\n              ></span>\n            </div>\n          </template>\n        </th>\n      </template>\n    </tr>\n  </template>\n</thead>\n```\n\nClicking a header now toggles between ascending, descending, and unsorted. Every other feature follows this same pattern: register the feature (and its row model factory as a slot on `tableFeatures` if it has one), then use the APIs it adds to the table, columns, and rows, attaching any interactive controls to real elements in your markup. See the [Sorting example](./examples/sorting) for custom sort functions, multi-sorting, and per-column options.\n\n## Where to Go Next\n\n**Table state.** In v9, table state is backed by TanStack Store atoms, which the adapter makes reactive in Alpine. You usually do not need to manage it yourself. Set `initialState` for starting values and call feature APIs like `table.setSorting(...)` or `table.nextPage()`. When you need to read a state slice in your markup, use `table.atoms.<slice>.get()` (for example `table.atoms.pagination.get().pageIndex`) or `table.store.get()` for the whole state. There is no state selector, because the table instance is already reactive. The [Table State Guide](./guide/table-state.md) is the foundational guide for everything else.\n\n**Feature examples.** Each feature has a runnable example, such as [Column Filters](./examples/filters), [Pagination](./examples/pagination), [Row Selection](./examples/row-selection), and [Column Visibility](./examples/column-visibility).\n\n**Composable tables.** When multiple tables in your app share features and row models, define them once with `createTableHook`:\n\n```ts\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  sortFns,\n})\n\nconst { createAppTable, createAppColumnHelper } = createTableHook({ features })\n```\n\nThen call `createAppTable({ columns, data })` from your component instead of `createTable`, and define columns with `createAppColumnHelper`. See the [Basic createAppTable example](./examples/basic-app-table) for the full pattern.\n\n**Examples.** Browse the runnable [Alpine examples](./examples/basic-create-table), from basic tables to feature demos, to see intended usage end to end.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Quick Start\n---\n\nTanStack Table is a headless table library. It manages your table's state and logic (sorting, filtering, pagination, selection, and more) while you keep 100% control over the markup and styles. This page gets you from install to a rendering Alpine table, then shows how to layer on your first feature.\n\n## Installation\n\n```bash\nnpm install @tanstack/alpine-table alpinejs\n```\n\nThe `@tanstack/alpine-table` package works with Alpine 3.\n\n## How the Alpine adapter works\n\nThe adapter is built around two ideas:\n\n- **`createTable` returns a reactive table instance.** State lives in [TanStack Store](https://tanstack.com/store/latest) atoms that the adapter bridges into Alpine's reactivity. Any Alpine binding that reads a table API (`table.getRowModel()`, `table.atoms.sorting.get()`, and so on) re-runs automatically when the underlying state changes. There is no state selector to pass.\n- **You render with `x-html` and `table.FlexRender`.** A column's `cell`/`header`/`footer` renderer returns a string of HTML. `table.FlexRender({ cell })` produces that string and you place it with `x-html`.\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> Alpine does not initialize directives (`@click`, `x-model`, etc.) inside content set with `x-html`. So render cell/header **content** with `x-html=\"table.FlexRender(...)\"`, but put any **interactivity** (click-to-sort, filter inputs, checkboxes) on real elements in your markup, next to the `x-html` span. You will see this pattern in the sorting example below.\n\n## Your First Table\n\nYou define the table in a JavaScript module with [`Alpine.data`](https://alpinejs.dev/globals/alpine-data), then render it from your HTML.\n\n```ts\n// main.ts\nimport Alpine from 'alpinejs'\nimport { FlexRender, createTable, tableFeatures } from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\nimport type { ColumnDef } from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\n\n// 1. Define the shape of your data\ntype Person = {\n  firstName: string\n  lastName: string\n  age: number\n}\n\nconst defaultData: Array<Person> = [\n  { firstName: 'tanner', lastName: 'linsley', age: 24 },\n  { firstName: 'tandy', lastName: 'miller', age: 40 },\n  { firstName: 'joe', lastName: 'dirte', age: 45 },\n]\n\n// 2. New in v9: declare which features this table uses (none yet)\nconst features = tableFeatures({})\n\n// 3. Define your columns. Renderers return HTML strings (rendered via x-html).\nconst columns: Array<ColumnDef<typeof features, Person>> = [\n  {\n    accessorKey: 'firstName', // accessorKey shorthand\n    header: 'First Name',\n    cell: (info) => info.getValue(),\n  },\n  {\n    accessorFn: (row) => row.lastName, // accessorFn alternative with a custom id\n    id: 'lastName',\n    header: () => '<span>Last Name</span>',\n    cell: (info) => `<i>${info.getValue<string>()}</i>`,\n  },\n  {\n    accessorKey: 'age',\n    header: () => 'Age',\n  },\n]\n\n// 4. Register an Alpine component\nAlpine.data('table', () => {\n  // Store data in Alpine-reactive state so updates flow into the table\n  const local = Alpine.reactive({ data: defaultData })\n\n  // 5. Create the table instance, reading data through a getter so it stays reactive\n  const table = createTable({\n    features,\n    columns,\n    get data() {\n      return local.data\n    },\n  })\n\n  // Expose the instance (and FlexRender) to the template\n  return { table, FlexRender }\n})\n\nwindow.Alpine = Alpine\nAlpine.start()\n```\n\n```html\n<!-- index.html -->\n<div x-data=\"table\">\n  <table>\n    <thead>\n      <template\n        x-for=\"headerGroup in table.getHeaderGroups()\"\n        :key=\"headerGroup.id\"\n      >\n        <tr>\n          <template x-for=\"header in headerGroup.headers\" :key=\"header.id\">\n            <th>\n              <template x-if=\"!header.isPlaceholder\">\n                <span x-html=\"FlexRender({ header })\"></span>\n              </template>\n            </th>\n          </template>\n        </tr>\n      </template>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <template x-for=\"row in table.getRowModel().rows\" :key=\"row.id\">\n        <tr>\n          <template x-for=\"cell in row.getAllCells()\" :key=\"cell.id\">\n            <td x-html=\"FlexRender({ cell })\"></td>\n          </template>\n        </tr>\n      </template>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n<script type=\"module\" src=\"/main.ts\"></script>\n```\n\nA few things to note:\n\n- `tableFeatures({})` declares which optional features the table uses. Registering only what you need keeps bundles small and gives TypeScript accurate types for the table instance.\n- The core row model is always included automatically. Feature row models (sorting, filtering, pagination) are registered as slots directly on the `tableFeatures({...})` call when you need them.\n- The `get data()` getter keeps the table reactive. When `local.data` is reassigned, the table sees the new data. Passing `data: local.data` would capture a one-time snapshot.\n- `FlexRender` is also attached to the instance as `table.FlexRender`, so you can write `x-html=\"table.FlexRender({ cell })\"` instead of exposing the top-level helper.\n\nSee the full [Basic createTable example](./examples/basic-create-table) for a runnable version with more columns and a footer.\n\n## Add a Feature: Sorting\n\nFeatures are opt-in in v9. To make columns sortable, register `rowSortingFeature` and the `sortedRowModel` factory in `tableFeatures`, then wire a header click handler. Because the click handler cannot live inside `x-html`, wrap the rendered header in a real element and attach `@click` there.\n\n```ts\n// main.ts (additions)\nimport {\n  FlexRender,\n  createSortedRowModel,\n  createTable,\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortFns,\n  tableFeatures,\n} from '@tanstack/alpine-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature, // enables sorting APIs and state\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(), // client-side sorting\n  sortFns,\n})\n\n// columns and the Alpine.data registration are otherwise unchanged\n```\n\n```html\n<!-- index.html: the <thead> becomes -->\n<thead>\n  <template\n    x-for=\"headerGroup in table.getHeaderGroups()\"\n    :key=\"headerGroup.id\"\n  >\n    <tr>\n      <template x-for=\"header in headerGroup.headers\" :key=\"header.id\">\n        <th>\n          <template x-if=\"!header.isPlaceholder\">\n            <div\n              :style=\"header.column.getCanSort() ? 'cursor: pointer' : ''\"\n              @click=\"header.column.getToggleSortingHandler()?.($event)\"\n            >\n              <span x-html=\"FlexRender({ header })\"></span\n              ><span\n                x-text=\"({ asc: ' 🔼', desc: ' 🔽' })[header.column.getIsSorted()] ?? ''\"\n              ></span>\n            </div>\n          </template>\n        </th>\n      </template>\n    </tr>\n  </template>\n</thead>\n```\n\nClicking a header now toggles between ascending, descending, and unsorted. Every other feature follows this same pattern: register the feature (and its row model factory as a slot on `tableFeatures` if it has one), then use the APIs it adds to the table, columns, and rows, attaching any interactive controls to real elements in your markup. See the [Sorting example](./examples/sorting) for custom sort functions, multi-sorting, and per-column options.\n\n## Where to Go Next\n\n**Table state.** In v9, table state is backed by TanStack Store atoms, which the adapter makes reactive in Alpine. You usually do not need to manage it yourself. Set `initialState` for starting values and call feature APIs like `table.setSorting(...)` or `table.nextPage()`. When you need to read a state slice in your markup, use `table.atoms.<slice>.get()` (for example `table.atoms.pagination.get().pageIndex`) or `table.store.get()` for the whole state. There is no state selector, because the table instance is already reactive. The [Table State Guide](./guide/table-state.md) is the foundational guide for everything else.\n\n**Feature examples.** Each feature has a runnable example, such as [Column Filters](./examples/filters), [Pagination](./examples/pagination), [Row Selection](./examples/row-selection), and [Column Visibility](./examples/column-visibility).\n\n**Composable tables.** When multiple tables in your app share features and row models, define them once with `createTableHook`:\n\n```ts\nconst features = tableFeatures({\n  rowSortingFeature,\n  sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel(),\n  sortFns,\n})\n\nconst { createAppTable, createAppColumnHelper } = createTableHook({ features })\n```\n\nThen call `createAppTable({ columns, data })` from your component instead of `createTable`, and define columns with `createAppColumnHelper`. See the [Basic createAppTable example](./examples/basic-app-table) for the full pattern.\n\n**Examples.** Browse the runnable [Alpine examples](./examples/basic-create-table), from basic tables to feature demos, to see intended usage end to end.\n"}
